  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "If ${8}^{5a-12}=\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}}$ then $a=$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${8}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{({8}^{2})}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{8}^{2(a+4)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & ={8}^{-2(a+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(8)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-12 & =-2(a+4) \\\\ 5a-12 & =-2a-8 \\\\ 7a & =4 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
